<!--
  A fictitious secure file exchange gateway's audit export, written to exercise the AI translations on
  XML that is harder than a flat list of same-shaped records:

  * Records sit at depth 3 (export > batch > record), not directly under the root.
  * Context a record needs lives on its ancestors' attributes: the batch's node, site and time zone.
    Policy changes carry a local time with no offset, so only the batch's timeZone places them.
  * Many record shapes in one stream: authentication, transfer, share, policy change, admin action,
    session end, heartbeat, diagnostic and a batch summary trailer.
  * Two namespaces besides the default one (dlp: and xsi:), with xsi:nil for absent old values.
  * Schema drift: the second batch (schemaVersion 3.1) puts the user on attributes rather than child
    elements, writes durations in seconds rather than ISO 8601, and adds IPv6 clients.
  * Repeated and nested lists: MFA factors, files with DLP findings with matched rules, recipients by
    role, approval chains, changed settings.
  * Escaping: entities, character references, CDATA that contains markup-like text, non-ASCII names.
  * Records that are not events: heartbeats, a diagnostic and the batch summaries.

  All names, hosts and addresses are invented; IPs are from the documentation ranges (RFC 5737/3849).
-->
